==== SD Card Installation ====

Prepare the microSD card by extracting the image onto the device. Replace ///dev/mmcblkX// with the correct device for the SD card:

<code>
gzip -cd bananapi_bpi-r2-sdcard.img.gz > /dev/mmcblkX
</code>

This card can now be used to boot OpenWrt recovery (==initramfs) as well as production (==with f2fs overlay) system from that card.
Install of the bootloader and recovery system to the on-board eMMC can be triggered by setting a bootloader environment variable or by using the bootloader menu accessible via the serial console.

To access the boot menu, connect your serial interface (e.g., an FT232) to the debug UART pins (three pins labelled TX, RX, GND next to the SATA ports). Don't forget to cross lines: TX of the serial interface should be connected to RX of the router, and vice versa.
The following command can be used to connect to the serial console:

<code>
picocom -b 115200 /dev/ttyUSB0
</code>

It can be a bit disconcerting to power up the BPI-R2. After plugging in the microSD card and connecting the power cable, the PWR switch (next to the SATA ports) has to be pressed for several seconds. First, all three LEDs light up. However, keep holding PWR until the lights begin flashing.

To write OpenWrt onto the eMMC, select corresponding boot option from the menu via arrow keys and hit enter.

At the next reboot the bootloader on the SD card will write ARM Trusted Firmware, U-Boot and the OpenWrt recovery image contained on the SD card to the eMMC. You can then switch to boot from eMMC and install a production image using sysupgrade.


=== sysupgrade ===

Login as root via SSH on 192.168.1.1, then enter the following commands:

<code>
cd /tmp
wget https://downloads.openwrt.org/snapshots/targets/mediatek/mt7623/openwrt-mediatek-mt7623-bananapi_bpi-r2-squashfs-sysupgrade.itb
</code>

You can alternatively push the //sysupgrade// image from your PC via SSH if you have not yet connected the router to the internet:

<code>
scp -O openwrt-23.05.2-mediatek-mt7623-bananapi_bpi-r2-squashfs-sysupgrade.itb root@192.168.1.1:/tmp/
</code>

Then install the SquashFS image via //sysupgrade// on the router:

<code>
cd /tmp
sysupgrade /tmp/openwrt-mediatek-mt7623-bananapi_bpi-r2-squashfs-sysupgrade.itb
</code>


